  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 336 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nThe rest of the work week looks to continue to be similar to\ntoday and tomorrow, but with slightly less cloud cover and a\ngradual warming into the weekend. The GFS and EPS ensembles are\nshowing that a mid/upper-level ridge is expected to build over\nthe Midwest by the weekend. This would bring southerly flow back\nto the area, possibly increasing temperatures early next week.\nThere remains uncertainty in the exact magnitude of the warm up\nhowever. NBM and LREF interquartile ranges for Saturday through\nTuesday are around 7-8 degrees, so guidance is unsure if we will\nremain in the mid to upper 70s or heat up into the mid 80s.\nThese wide ranges are driven by a return of rain chances which\ncould cool temperatures as low as the mid 60s (10% percentile).\nThe NBM does hint at a possible return to the 90s with the 75th\npercentile for Tuesdays high temperature being in the upper 80s\nto low 90s. Long-range deterministic models are also hinting of\na deep trough moving through the central Great Plains on Tuesday\nthat could bring a cold front to the area Tuesday into\nWednesday, but ensembles during the same time are having a\ndifficult time capturing the timing of the front with\ninterquartile ranges around 10 degrees by the middle of next\nweek; temperatures could remain in the 80s or drop back into the\n70s.\n\nWith the approaching warm up, our chances of rain increase\nagain. The LREF is showing a 40-70% chance of some rainfall\nacross central and northeastern MO over the weekend. Guidance\nduring the last several days has been pushing these rain chances\nfarther east into our area with each model run. The possible\ncold front during the middle of the week also brings the\ngreatest chance of widespread rain through the forecast period.\nLREF guidance shows a 70-80% chance of rain across our entire\narea.",
